      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "Label": "UNLABELLED",
          "content": "Coronary stent imaging with computed tomography is challenging because of high-density artifacts. However, noninvasive coronary angiography with computed tomography is gaining acceptance as a valid alternative to cardiac catheterization in a broader array of clinical settings, and the work-up of patients after coronary stent implantation represents an application of pressing clinical utility. Only a minority of patients who develop recurrent chest pain after stent implantation have myocardial ischemia, thus a sensitive noninvasive study is desirable. With an awareness of the limitations of the technique, the systematic application of dedicated strategies of data post-processing and display techniques permits partial compensation of the technical limitations brought about by metallic struts."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "ADVANCES IN KNOWLEDGE",
          "content": "1. The role of coronary computed tomography angiography in the diagnostic work-up of patients with symptoms after stent placement 2. Systematization of post-processing, display, and review techniques for optimal evaluation of coronary stents with coronary computed tomography angiography."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "SUMMARY STATEMENT",
          "content": "The follow-up of patients after coronary stenting is an appealing but challenging application of coronary computed tomography angiography. The presence of intrinsic limitations requires the use of dedicated post-processing and visualization techniques."
        }
      ]},
